# Caddy terminates TLS for the stack itself.
#
# It has to. The stack is meant to run on the lab's own hardware as readily as on
# a rented box, and a LAN has no provider load balancer to terminate TLS in front
# of it. docs/plan/architecture.md section 3.
#
# What answers on the hostname differs between a deployment and a laptop, and so
# does the scheme, so a route file in caddy/routes/ opens the whole site block
# and this file imports one of them here at the top level.
#
# Why the site block is not in this file with only its routes imported, which is
# the shape this had until the development profile went to plain HTTP: a site
# address written http:// cannot carry a tls directive, and a file imported
# inside a site block cannot open a site of its own. Two other arrangements were
# weighed. A second whole Caddyfile, chosen by a compose bind, repeats everything
# the two profiles have in common rather than the one line they differ on. Making
# the site address a variable and moving tls into the route file also works, and
# it costs a second variable that has to agree with the first, so COMPOSE_PROFILES
# could say development while the other one still said https. One switch that
# cannot disagree with itself is worth repeating four lines of health route.
#
# compose.yaml sets ORO_ROUTES, and it is "deployment" unless COMPOSE_PROFILES
# asked for something else. A name with no file beside it in caddy/routes/ stops
# Caddy at startup, which the healthcheck reports, rather than serving the
# hostname from a configuration nobody chose.
import /etc/caddy/routes/{$ORO_ROUTES}.caddyfile
